# Per-minute poll for settled payments.
#
# WHY A MINUTE IS AFFORDABLE, and it is NOT the SOP's reason. The SOP can end a run at its first
# step because it works from a list of open references, and usually there are none.
# `confirm_settlements.py` has no such concept: it inverted the question precisely to avoid needing
# one, so it has no early exit at all. Every tick reads the signature window and then fetches each
# transaction in it the ledger has not recorded. Measured on the box 2026-08-16, that was 17 RPC
# calls a tick and 8,126 a day, 11 of the 15 fetches re-deriving a verdict about the same unrelated
# transactions -- a verdict that, on a finalized transaction, cannot change.
#
# The cadence is affordable because the confirmer caches those permanent negatives. A tick that
# finds nothing new costs the two cheap reads and no transaction fetches at all, so the cost scales
# with what actually arrived rather than with the size of the window.
#
# WHY NOT SLOWER. The claim this exists to support is that the owner learns of a payment within
# about a minute of it settling on chain -- stated in QUICKSTART.md, the SOP, and the write-up. A
# five-minute tick would make that sentence false, and the sentence is the deliverable. It is also
# why the fix for the cost was a cache rather than a backoff: a backoff buys quiet by delaying a real
# payment's receipt, which is the one thing here that is not ours to trade.
#
# Persistent=false ON PURPOSE. After downtime there is no value in firing a catch-up run
# immediately: the next ordinary tick is at most a minute away, and a catch-up burst on boot would
# announce a backlog in one clump rather than as it settles. The ledger already prevents duplicates,
# so this is about presentation rather than correctness.

[Unit]
Description=Poll for settled payments once a minute
Documentation=file:%h/.config/systemd/user/zc-announce.service

[Timer]
OnBootSec=2min
OnUnitActiveSec=1min
AccuracySec=10s
Persistent=false
Unit=zc-announce.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
